  "worked_solution": "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $13$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(1)(1)=5>0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$",
